From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 10:47:02 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Resolve each chunk once for the leaf decay scan

BlockLeaves.updateTick reads a 9x9x9 cube - 729 blocks - to decide
whether one leaf block is still connected to a log, and every one of
those reads hashed the chunk coordinates again. A live capture put 13 of
the 18 samples that landed in leaf decay inside those reads.

The cube spans at most four chunks. Walking z before y makes the chunk
constant for a whole inner run, so the scan resolves it on the order of
twenty times instead of 729. The scan writes a distinct cell of
surroundings for every (k1, l1, i2) and never reads one back, so the
order it visits them in is not observable; BlockLeavesScanOrderTest
pins that the index formula is injective over the cube and that the cube
really does stay inside four chunks wherever it is centred.

getTypeIfLoaded gains a form that takes the chunk, which is its old body
with the lookup lifted out. The BlockPosition form now delegates to it,
which means the chunk is looked up before the captured block states are
consulted rather than after - a pure map read either way.

The bounds check ahead of the scan built two BlockPositions per decay
check; areChunksLoadedBetween forwards straight to isAreaLoaded with the
same six coordinates, so it calls that instead.

Verified differentially: two 70-second runs of the same jar on a fixed
seed differ in 1 of 625 chunks, which is the noise floor from tick
timing. Against a build from before this change the new jar differs in
1 chunk on one run and 0 on the other - indistinguishable from that
floor, over 70 seconds of live leaf decay.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/BlockLeaves.java b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/BlockLeaves.java
